#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Duty Board v3.146.0 — POS 2: The Point of Sale Profile
(pass 2 of 8).

The track's first TEN-chapter deep module: the counter's
constitution — the groundwork checklist, items/prices/tax, all five
profile tabs at field depth (setup, the discount constitution,
payments and returns, the voucher tab's conservatism, integrations),
the cashiers' access boxes normalised by mode, and the Power
Approver table. 35-bank.

Deploy: apply -> commit -> then on the server:
  bench --site xlevel.clouderp.one execute duty_board.academy_seed_pos_pro.seed_pos_pro_track

Anchored, idempotent. Requires v3.145.0.
Run from ~/frappe-bench/apps/duty_board.
"""

import io
import json
import os
import sys

INIT = "duty_board/__init__.py"
DATA_PATH = "duty_board/academy_pos_pro_data.json"
SEEDER_PATH = "duty_board/academy_seed_pos_pro.py"
CHECK_ONLY = "--check" in sys.argv

L = lambda t, est, html: {"title": t, "est": est, "html": html}
Q = lambda q, opts, ans, why, src: {"q": q, "opts": opts, "ans": ans, "why": why, "src": src}

LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — One record, one counter: the constitution", "12", "<p>The deep module opens on the record everything else in this track obeys: the POINT OF SALE PROFILE — one record governing one counter's entire behaviour, its settings grouped into five tabs, every field a policy with an owner. The constitution chapter.</p><p><b>The constitutional frame.</b> The academy's settings-walk discipline, arriving at its richest single record: the profile is walked field by field at implementation (every consequential default converted from an accident into a decision — the defaults-are-decisions law of the Sysadmin track, at the counter), its answers recorded with their reasons and deciders (the configuration record's counter page), changes travel the change ceremony (the profile edit is a policy edit — the discount mode switched casually is a constitutional amendment made in a corridor), and the corridor toggle stands convicted in advance in its counter clothes: the return window 'extended for one customer', the override requirement 'lifted for the busy season', the negative-stock permission 'enabled temporarily' — each an amendment that computes for every sale thereafter, remembered by no one, found by the walk or by the loss.</p><p><b>Profile per counter, or per shop?</b> The module's first design decision, taken from the manual's own guidance and given the academy's reasoning: ONE PROFILE PER COUNTER where tills need different hardware behaviour, different opening floats or different permissions; ONE SHARED PROFILE where every till in a shop should behave identically — far less to maintain, with terminals still distinguished individually for reporting. The decision test, stated for the consultant: the profile count follows the POLICY count, not the terminal count (three identical tills under one policy is one profile; the till by the door that also handles returns is a second policy and earns its own record) — the role-catalogue logic of the Sysadmin track, at the counter: few records, purpose-clear, each one a policy-shape that exists because a policy-shape exists.</p><p><b>The module's map.</b> Ten chapters, the track's first deep module: the groundwork the profile points at (2), the items, prices and tax beneath it (3), Tab one's setup fields (4), Tab two — the discount constitution, the profile's most consequential ground (5), Tab three — payments, returns, lay-by and staff purchase (6), Tab four — the voucher tab's deliberate conservatism (7), Tab five plus the users table and the Power Approvers (8), Okelewo's profiles built (9), and the patterns and the law (10). One voice discipline governs throughout, inherited and enforced: the profile's fields are decided by their OWNING functions (the discount mode is a margin policy — selling's owner decides; the cash-refund switch is a treasury policy; the voucher caps are the money seat's) with the administrator holding the pen — the configure authority executing decisions, never making them, which is the authority map of two tracks, at the record where its breach is most tempting because the pen sits so close to the counter's daily noise.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The groundwork: ten records before one profile", "13", "<p>The profile points at records that must already exist — and the manual's warning is this chapter's thesis: get these right first and the profile takes ten minutes; get them wrong and you will be unpicking postings for a week. The groundwork chapter.</p><p><b>The checklist, in order.</b> The ten records, walked with their reasons: the <b>COMPANY</b> (the legal entity that will own the sales — its default currency and chart confirmed: the counter posts into the Accounts track's world, and posts into the WRONG company are the week of unpicking incarnate), the <b>COST CENTRE</b> (where the shop's trading lands — one per branch as the chains' standard: the dimension the Accounts track's branch views harvest), the <b>WAREHOUSE</b> (the stock location the till sells from — a LEAF warehouse, never a group: the till can only sell what this warehouse holds, and the leaf-not-group rule is the Inventory track's custody law expressed as a validation), the <b>BRANCH</b> (the shop itself — the record a terminal gets LOCKED to, its three fields tying company, warehouse and cost centre into one anchor: named clearly enough that nobody commissioning a terminal picks the wrong one, because the wrong pick is an administrator-verified unlock away from correction), the <b>PRICE LIST</b> (selling, in the company currency, with rates loaded — Chapter 3's validity discipline), the <b>TAX TEMPLATE</b> (the profile will not save without one — the statutory carrier, per Chapter 3), the <b>PAYMENT METHODS</b> (one record per way money arrives — Chapter 3's special three), the <b>CUSTOMER GROUP and TERRITORY</b> defaults (where walk-ins are filed — the master hygiene of the data tracks, pre-decided), the <b>WALK-IN CUSTOMER</b> (the record unnamed sales book to — one, deliberate, not a per-cashier improvisation), and the <b>USER ACCOUNTS</b> (one per cashier, each with a working email and a password they know — module 1's one-person-one-account law as a checklist line, with the email load-bearing because the one-time-code machinery of Chapter 8 delivers to it).</p><p><b>The sanity check.</b> The manual's own pre-commissioning discipline, adopted as the academy's rehearsal instinct: before commissioning a terminal, sell ONE item end to end from the back office against the same warehouse and tax template — if that posts cleanly, the till will too. The check's logic taught, not just its steps: the till is a fast front end onto the same posting machinery, so the back-office sale exercises the whole chain (the item, the price, the tax, the warehouse, the accounts) with none of the till's variables — the failure isolated to the groundwork BEFORE the terminal adds its own layer, which is the nothing-first-in-production law wearing retail clothes.</p><p><b>The groundwork's custody.</b> The chapter's closing split, per the standing map: every checklist record has its OWNING track and pen (the company and chart are the accountant's, the warehouse and branch inventory's, the price list selling's, the accounts wiring finance's) — the POS implementation does not CREATE this world; it points a counter at a world seven tracks already govern, and the consultant who finds the groundwork absent has found the engagement's real scope: the counter that seems to need a till often needs a chart, a warehouse discipline, and a price list first, and the certified professional prices the foundation before the furniture.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Items, prices, tax & tender: the counter's raw materials", "13", "<p>Beneath the profile, the records every scan touches: the items with their flags, the prices with their windows, the tax template the bill is computed by, and the payment methods money arrives through. The raw-materials chapter.</p><p><b>Items: the flags that matter at the till.</b> The item record's counter-consequential fields: the BARCODE (if the shop scans) and a sensible SHORT NAME (long names truncate in the cart — legibility as configuration), the <b>TAXABLE flag</b> (on for standard-rated goods, off for exempt lines — honoured PER LINE by the till: the mixed basket taxed correctly because each line carries its own answer), the <b>BATCH/SERIAL setup</b> (an item tracked by batch or serial must be BORN that way in the item record, or the till cannot ask the cashier which batch or serial is being sold — the tracking decision is a birth decision, per the master tracks' law), NON-STOCK items supported (services and fees, unheld by stock checks), and KIOSK flags (the items shown when a terminal switches to kiosk mode).</p><p><b>Prices: the validity-window discipline.</b> The pricing rules with the disease they prevent: rates loaded against the profile's selling price list (an item with NO rate scans at ZERO — flagged by the till but not blocked: the zero-rated scan being the counter's classic silent leak, sold at nil while everyone assumes a price), the <b>validity window</b> read properly (a rate whose start date has not begun, or whose end date has passed, is treated as NO RATE AT ALL — the manual's own diagnosis for 'items ringing up at nil', and this track's standing first-check for pricing complaints), BATCH prices preferred over the list rate where a batch carries its own, and the refresh discipline known (price changes reach terminals on the next price refresh — no restarts, per module 1's retired superstition).</p><p><b>Tax: one template, one behaviour.</b> The template law, taught with its trap: the profile names a sales tax template, and tax is calculated BY THE TEMPLATE at the moment the bill is created — with two interacting settings: <b>Is Tax Inclusive</b> on the profile (shelf prices already contain tax) and the per-item Taxable flag. The trap is structural and the manual names it plainly: switching the inclusive setting REWRITES THE SHARED TEMPLATE (its lines marked as included in the printed rate, or reversed) — so two profiles with OPPOSITE tax treatments pointed at the SAME template will fight over it, each save rewriting the other's behaviour: <b>give each treatment its own template</b> — the one-template-one-behaviour law, and a first-class example of this academy's blast-radius instinct: the setting that silently edits a shared record is a setting whose radius must be known before it is touched.</p><p><b>Payment methods: the special three.</b> Every tender is a payment method record added to the profile's payments table — the TABLE ORDER being the DISPLAY ORDER cashiers see (a policy, not an accident: cash first because cash is fastest is a decision someone makes) — and three names carry special behaviour: <b>Cash</b> (prompts for the amount tendered, calculates change, opens the drawer, works OFFLINE), <b>Store Credit</b> (draws down the customer's balance — requires the server to confirm it, so UNAVAILABLE offline: the balance that cannot be verified from a local copy, the design honesty module 8 builds on), and <b>Gift Voucher</b> (appears automatically when vouchers are enabled, positioned after cash and card — module 7's tender). Card, transfer and their kin simply RECORD the amount — no live call, so they work offline — with the integrated card terminal being the exception Chapter 8's integrations tab governs. The chapter's closing line: the raw materials are seven tracks' masters meeting one screen — and every counter complaint that begins 'the till is wrong' ends, four times in five, at a flag, a window, a template, or a table order that was never decided.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Tab one: setup — the counter's operating posture", "14", "<p>The first tab: the defaults, the cart's rules, the stock posture, the shift machinery, and the terminal switches — the counter's operating posture, field by field. The setup chapter.</p><p><b>Defaults.</b> The walk-in machinery's settings: the DEFAULT customer group and territory (where counter-created customers are filed — the master hygiene pre-decided so eleven cashiers file identically), <b>Allow Duplicate Customer Names</b> (OFF refuses a second customer with an existing name — the dedup-at-birth guard of the master tracks as a checkbox: turned on only where genuinely common names cause repeated blocks, and turned on as a DECISION with the merge tool's monthly scan as its compensating control), and Max Items in Search Results (the grid kept snappy on modest hardware — performance as configuration).</p><p><b>The cart's rules.</b> The basket's governance: <b>Allow Clear Cart</b> with its approver interplay read precisely (when OFF, the Clear button still appears IF a Power Approver holds the clear-cart permission — but requires approval: the design that makes 'discard this basket' an authorised event rather than an impossible one, because the clear-cart power matters to module 8's fraud map — the basket built, paid in cash, and cleared instead of completed is the quiet void's mechanism, and the approval requirement is its wall), <b>Clear Cart On Logout</b> (off where shifts hand over mid-basket; on where an abandoned basket must never carry to the next person — a custody decision), <b>Allow Checkout Item Update</b> (quantities changeable on the payment screen — convenience weighed against the review discipline of building the cart right), the <b>queue/stack toggle</b> (Enable Item Queue Options — stack merges a re-scan into the existing line; queue makes each scan its own line, suiting counters where each unit needs its own serial or batch), and the held-basket HOUSEKEEPING pair (Delete Cart Queue + Delete After days — the parked baskets aged out automatically: the museum discipline, at the hold shelf).</p><p><b>Inventory and stock.</b> The stock posture's three: <b>Allow Viewing Other Warehouses</b> (the 'do you have it at the other shop?' answered from Inventory Ops without a phone call — need-to-know widened deliberately, per branch design), <b>Allow Negative Stock</b> (selling what the system believes is absent — MIRRORING a company-wide stock setting and refusing to save unless that setting also permits it: the two-lock design read correctly — the profile cannot quietly grant what the Inventory track's constitution forbids, and the certified consultant treats the request to enable it as the Inventory-track conversation it actually is), and <b>Enable Auto Allocate Batch</b> (batches picked automatically, OLDEST FIRST, asking only when the quantity cannot be covered — FEFO as a speed setting: the busy counter's throughput bought at the cost of manual control, decided by the goods' nature — the pharmacy's expiry discipline wants it on; the counter selling mixed provenance may want the ask).</p><p><b>Shift, terminal and access.</b> The remaining posture: <b>Enable Shift Management</b> (a shift must open before selling and close with a counted drawer — module 4's whole ritual, switched here: the counter that handles cash turns it ON, and the consultant treats OFF as an exception needing a reason), <b>Enable Offline Login</b> (sign-in against the terminal's local record when the server is unreachable — prior successful online sign-in on THAT terminal required, expiring seven days after the last one: recommended ON for any shop with unreliable power or connectivity, because the cashier signed out when the line drops otherwise cannot sell at all), Enable Kiosk Mode, <b>Enable Messages</b> (the Message Hub — off at counters that should not handle transfer requests: the channel scoped per seat), <b>Allow Transaction Date Edit</b> (back-dating a sale, future dates never — granted SPARINGLY because it moves revenue between reporting days: the period-integrity law of the Accounts track guarding a checkbox), and Enable Airtime Sales (module 6's console, switched per counter). The chapter's cadence line: tab one is where the counter's daily FEEL is decided — and every field just walked is a feel someone will live with hourly, which is exactly why each one gets an owner and a reason before it gets a tick.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Tab two: the discount constitution", "14", "<p>The manual's own words set this chapter's weight: Discount Mode is <i>the single most consequential setting on the profile</i> — it decides how a cashier is allowed to take money off a line, which is to say it decides how the firm's margin is governed at the moment of pressure. The discount chapter.</p><p><b>The three modes.</b> Walked as the margin-governance designs they are: <b>STANDARD</b> — the cashier types a discount directly on the line, clearing it with an approver at the moment of discounting where an override is required (suits the small shop with the supervisor on the floor — governance by proximity, honest about its dependency: the mode works exactly as well as the approver's presence), <b>APPROVAL-BASED</b> — every discount must carry a REASON chosen from the markdown list plus a typed explanation, so nothing is discounted without a stated cause and the margin report tells you WHY (suits chains that need every markdown attributed — the mode this academy recommends as the trade default, because it converts the margin's leak from a number into a diagnosis: module 5 of this track builds the markdown-reason craft on it), and <b>CODE-BASED</b> — the cashier cannot type a discount at all; they raise a request from the cart, a category lead approves it centrally (optionally changing the amount), and the cashier applies the returned code, with every stage timestamped and the code reserved against that cashier so two tills cannot consume one approval (suits high-value goods where every concession is negotiated — the strongest separation, at the cost of round-trip latency, and the full cycle is module 5's terrain). The selection judgment stated: the mode follows the GOODS and the FLOOR (the supermarket's velocity, the boutique's negotiation, the electronics counter's ticket sizes), it is chosen by the margin's owner, and it is the profile's one field the consultant never lets default.</p><p><b>The override economics.</b> The authorisation channel's honest trade, from the manual's own comparison: <b>Password</b> approval is faster and works OFFLINE, but requires the approver physically present; <b>One-Time Code</b> lets a manager approve from anywhere by reading six digits down the phone — but the terminal must be ONLINE to request one, every approver holding the permission receives the email, and codes expire after an hour. The design consequences drawn: the branch with a resident supervisor runs password; the branch whose approvers travel runs codes and accepts the online dependency; and the all-approvers-emailed behaviour is read as the audit feature it is — every code request is announced to every holder, so the quiet code extracted from one friendly approver does not exist as a category.</p><p><b>The remaining pricing fields.</b> The tab's other switches, each with its weight: <b>Allow Discount Edit</b> (the MASTER switch — off disables the discount control on every line regardless of mode: the counter that simply does not discount, expressed as one field), <b>Allow Rate Edit</b> and <b>Require Override for Rate Edit</b> (overwriting the unit price outright — the stronger power than discounting, marked on the line as Rate override for review, and the require-override companion treated as near-mandatory wherever rate editing is allowed at all: the unmarked rate change is the memory-price disease of module 1 readmitted through a checkbox), <b>Enable Promotions</b> and <b>Enable Promotion Code</b> (the automatic campaign machinery and its manual-code companion — module 5's promotions, switched here, with the lock-the-line behaviour previewed: a promotional discount on a line closes that line to further manual concession), <b>Is Tax Inclusive</b> (Chapter 3's shared-template law, cited not repeated), and <b>Allow Shipping Cost</b> (the editable delivery fee line — a priced service, not a discount vector, and watched as such). The chapter's closing conviction: the discount tab is where the margin's constitution lives — and the firm that cannot say which mode it runs, who approves, and by which channel has not chosen a discount policy; it has inherited one, and the inheritance is always the most permissive accident available.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Tab three: payments, returns, lay-by & staff purchase", "14", "<p>The third tab governs money's edge cases: the partial payment, the credit limit, the loyalty switch — and then the tab's heavy machinery: returns, lay-by and staff purchase, each a module of this track configured here. The money-edges chapter.</p><p><b>Customer and payment.</b> The four switches: <b>Allow Partial Payment</b> (a sale completing with less than full tender, the balance owing on the customer's account — the counter issuing CREDIT, which is the Selling track's credit discipline arriving at a checkbox: switched on only where the credit machinery behind it is real), <b>Enable Credit Limit Check</b> (the customer's balance and limit on the cart header, selling SUSPENDED when the limit is breached — 'Sales suspended due to customer outstanding' — releasable only by a Power Approver holding the credit-limit permission: the credit fence as physics, with the release as an authorised event), <b>Allow Customer Credit Against Invoice</b> (the unpaid balance carried on the invoice itself), and <b>Allow Loyalty Points</b> (earn and redeem at this counter — redemption calculated on the SERVER, so unavailable offline: the balance-verification honesty, again).</p><p><b>The returns block.</b> Money's reverse gear, configured conservatively: <b>Allow Cash Refund</b> (money back OUT of the drawer — when OFF, the only refund route is STORE CREDIT: the default posture this academy endorses for new deployments, because store credit keeps the refund inside the firm's ledger while cash refund opens the drawer to module 8's phantom-return mechanism; cash refund is enabled as a deliberate step with its override companion), <b>Allow Return Exchange</b> (goods swapped for goods, the difference settled either way), <b>Require Override for Returns</b> with its <b>Return Override Mode</b> (every return an authorised event — password or code, per Chapter 5's economics), and the <b>Global Return Policy</b> pair (the time limit enforced — Return Policy In Days, default thirty, with returns BEYOND the window still possible but requiring an override: the policy window as a soft fence with a governed gate, not a wall). The console-visibility rule read precisely, because it answers a standing support question: the Return &amp; Exchange console appears when ANY of cash refund, exchange or the global policy is enabled — none enabled, no console at all: the sidebar as the profile's mirror, one more time.</p><p><b>Lay-by.</b> The reservation machinery's three numbers: <b>Lay-By Deposit %</b> (the minimum taken at creation — default ten), <b>Lay-By Installments</b> (how many the balance splits into — default three), and <b>Lay-By Day Spacing</b> (comma-separated days from booking on which instalments fall due — the supplied 0, 30, 90 meaning now, a month, three months), with the reconciliation rule known: where spacing values and instalment count disagree, the COUNT wins and the schedule fills from the values available — provide as many spacings as instalments, and treat the mismatch as a configuration error to fix, not a behaviour to rely on. Module 6 runs the console; this tab decides its arithmetic — and the arithmetic is a CREDIT policy (the deposit is the firm's protection against the reserved-then-abandoned basket) decided by the margin's owner, not defaulted.</p><p><b>Staff purchase.</b> The two profile fields with their company-wide companions: <b>Enable Staff Purchase</b> (the console switched per counter) and <b>Staff Purchase Discount %</b> (applied automatically to every line — with the PRECEDENCE rule known: the per-profile percentage takes precedence over the company-wide staff discount at this counter), and the dependency map completed from the manual: employees need a PURCHASE LIMIT on their employee record, and the company-wide settings must name the employee purchase ACCOUNT and the payroll SALARY COMPONENT used for recovery — the Payroll track's variable-pay law wired into the counter's configuration, with module 6 running the flow. The chapter's closing frame: tab three is the counter's credit desk — every switch on it either lets money leave later, leave backwards, or leave discounted — and each one is therefore a treasury decision wearing a checkbox, owned accordingly.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Tab four: the voucher tab — deliberate conservatism", "13", "<p>The manual sets this tab's tone in one sentence, and the academy adopts it whole: gift vouchers are BEARER INSTRUMENTS — a customer buys one and anyone holding the code can spend it — so because they are money, everything here is deliberately conservative and OFF until you turn it on. The voucher-configuration chapter (the programme itself is module 7; this chapter is its constitution).</p><p><b>The core switches.</b> The tab's foundation: <b>Enable Gift Voucher</b> (the master switch — the voucher tile on the Sales Console and Gift Voucher as a payment method, positioned after cash and card), <b>Voucher Denominations</b> (the face values offered as quick buttons — comma-separated, with cashiers still able to type another amount: the presets as friction-reduction, not limits), <b>Enable Voucher Partial Redemption</b> (a voucher worth more than the bill leaving a BALANCE instead of being consumed whole), and the <b>Voucher Change Policy</b> (the surplus's fate when a voucher exceeds the bill: Remainder as Balance keeps it on the voucher; No Change forfeits it — a customer-fairness policy with a breakage consequence, decided by the programme's owner and printed into the customer's expectations).</p><p><b>The offline policy: the tab's hardest thinking.</b> The design taught as the exemplar it is: a voucher balance CANNOT be verified from a local copy — so <b>Voucher Offline Policy</b> defaults to <b>Online Only</b> (voucher activity refused while the terminal cannot reach the server, the cashier told plainly — the safe choice, and the refusal is intentional, not a defect), with <b>Guarded Offline</b> as the deliberate alternative: voucher activity permitted within TWO CAPS — the <b>Offline Max Value</b> (the largest single voucher redeemable offline) and the <b>Offline Daily Cap</b> (the total voucher value one terminal may redeem offline in a day). The design read in the academy's language: Guarded Offline is a RISK BUDGET — the firm pricing its maximum exposure to the double-spend window and buying continuity with it — and the certified consultant sets the caps in that conversation (what is the worst day worth?), never as round numbers, and revisits them as the programme's volumes grow.</p><p><b>The extended switches.</b> The remaining machinery, each off-by-default and each a policy: <b>Enable Voucher Top-up</b> (adding value to an existing voucher — the stored-value account deepened), <b>Enable Voucher Expiry</b> (vouchers carrying an expiry date and stopping after it — the breakage clock of module 7's accounting, switched here), <b>Enable Corporate Vouchers</b> (bulk issues to a named corporate customer — module 7's batch machinery serving them), <b>Enable Refund to Voucher</b> (a gift voucher as a refund destination on returns — the store-credit posture's sibling, keeping the refund as stored value), <b>Allow Voucher Buy-back</b> (the shop redeeming an unspent voucher for CASH — the tab's most dangerous switch, because it converts a bearer instrument directly into drawer cash: enabled only with the override and supervision machinery around it, and module 7's fraud map treats it as a named surface), and <b>Enforce Voucher Booking</b> (a voucher reserved centrally before issue at the counter — closing the window in which two tills could issue the SAME code: the double-issue race, closed by design, and the switch the multi-till shop turns on as a matter of course).</p><p><b>The tab's law.</b> The closing frame, joining Chapter 3's tender rules: unless Guarded Offline was deliberately chosen, any sale that either SELLS or REDEEMS a voucher is refused offline — and the cashier is told so plainly, which is the design's honesty doing the training. The voucher tab is the academy's conservatism made visible: every default protects the money, every enablement is a decision with an owner, and the consultant who finds this tab configured by enthusiasm — everything on, caps blank, buy-back live — has found module 7's first remediation item before the programme has sold a card.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Tab five, the users table & the Power Approvers", "14", "<p>The constitution's final ground: the integrations tab, the cashiers' access rows, and the approver table that makes override a governed event. The access chapter.</p><p><b>Tab five: integrations.</b> The card terminal's wiring, with its commissioning discipline: <b>Enable Payment Gateway</b> (the integrated terminal — a card payment PUSHED to the device, the till waiting for the result rather than the cashier keying it), the <b>Live Environment</b> switch with the manual's own ceremony adopted verbatim: commission with it OFF (pointing at the test environment), PROVE a test transaction, THEN switch — the nothing-first-in-production law at the payment rail, and the switch's state recorded in the change log both times; the <b>credentials</b> (merchant identifier, public key, private key — the private key stored MASKED, and all three under the Sysadmin track's credential custody: governed storage, rotation by events, never ambient), and the <b>Terminals table</b> (one row per physical card terminal, so the till knows which device receives a payment — the estate's map, per counter). The offline honesty repeated where it bites: an integrated card payment needs a live call — offline, the cashier is told to use cash, a manually keyed card entry, or transfer.</p><p><b>The users table.</b> One row per person who signs in at this counter, each carrying the account and its ACCESS BOXES: <b>Standard</b> (the Sales Console — ordinary selling), <b>Order</b> and <b>Checkout</b> (Pharmacy mode's two stages — with Checkout also granting Revenue Intelligence: the money seat sees the money reports, by design), <b>Booking</b> and <b>Cashier</b> (Booking mode's pair), and <b>Inventory Ops</b> (stock enquiry, material requests and transfers — on by default). The table's mechanics known precisely: the boxes are NORMALISED against the profile's mode on save (Standard mode grants everyone Standard and clears the two-stage boxes; Pharmacy clears the booking boxes — which is WHY module 1 said mode is settled first), one row per profile is marked as the user's DEFAULT (the profile they sign into automatically), and the LANDING rule known for support: the till opens on the first console the person is entitled to — Sales in Standard, Checkout-or-Order in Pharmacy, Cashier-or-Booking in Booking, and someone with only Inventory Ops lands there. The row is the counter's access register entry — granted by events, reconciled at the walk, per two tracks' standing law.</p><p><b>The Power Approvers.</b> The override machinery, complete: one row per approver, ticking ONLY the permissions they should hold — <b>Discount</b>, <b>Returns</b> (including returns outside the policy window), <b>Clear Cart</b>, <b>Rate</b>, and <b>Credit Limit Override</b> — five separate boxes, per module 1's per-permission precision. The prompt's mechanics: the approver's EMAIL and PASSWORD at the till, or the ONE-TIME CODE per the action's override mode — six digits, emailed to EVERY approver holding the relevant permission, expiring after an hour. And the failure mode the manual names, adopted as a commissioning check: <b>no approvers, no override</b> — if a profile requires an override for an action but no approver holds that permission, the control is DISABLED and the cashier is told there are no approvers configured: always add at least one, and the certified consultant's go-live checklist tests every override path with a real approval before the first customer, because the discount that cannot be approved at 6 p.m. on launch day is a queue, a scene, and a corridor toggle waiting to be offered.</p><p><b>The constitution, closed.</b> The chapter ends the walk with the module's discipline restated once: five tabs, one users table, one approver table — every field now walked has an owner, a reason, and a recorded answer, and the profile that emerges is not a configuration but a CONSTITUTION: the counter's policy, written where the machinery enforces it. Chapter 9 watches Okelewo write theirs.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Case study: Okelewo writes its constitution", "16", "<p>Three counters, three profiles, one afternoon of decisions that will govern years. Every chapter clocks in.</p><p><b>The groundwork walked (Ch. 2-3).</b> The ten-record checklist runs first and finds the expected gaps: the Lagos shop has no leaf warehouse (a group warehouse stood in — corrected through Inventory's owner), the price list carries forty items with EXPIRED validity windows (the zero-rated-scan disease, latent — the windows fixed and the quarterly price-list review added to selling's calendar), and the tax check catches the module's signature trap in the wild: the draft plan pointed BOTH the tax-inclusive flagship AND the tax-exclusive Lagos counter at ONE template — split into two before the first save, the one-template-one-behaviour law applied on paper instead of discovered in production. The sanity-check sale posts cleanly from the back office at each branch; the payments table is ordered deliberately (cash, transfer, card — the floor's actual frequency); and eleven user accounts exist with working emails before any profile names them.</p><p><b>The profiles decided (Ch. 1, 4-6).</b> The profile count follows the policy count: THREE profiles — one per shop, shared across each shop's tills (identical policy within a branch; the flagship's returns-handling till deferred as a possible fourth when module 5's volumes justify it). Tab one's posture: shift management ON everywhere (the counted drawer is the engagement's spine), offline login ON (Abeokuta's power reality), auto-allocate batch ON at the flagship's provisions counter (FEFO for the expiring goods) and OFF at the Lagos electronics wall (serials want the ask), transaction-date edit OFF for everyone (the period-integrity default, with the MD's one 'but sometimes—' answered by the reporting-days argument and withdrawn), duplicate-name refusal ON with the monthly merge scan as its companion. Tab two: <b>Approval-based discount mode</b> at all three (the margin diagnosed, not just spent — markdown reasons drafted for module 5: damaged, price match, display, expiring, goodwill — five, not thirty), password override at the two Abeokuta shops (resident supervisors), one-time codes at Lagos (the travelling approver reality, online dependency accepted and noted). Tab three: cash refund OFF at launch (store-credit-only — the conservative posture, revisited at ninety days), returns override REQUIRED with the thirty-day window, lay-by configured 15% / 3 instalments / 0-30-60 (the deposit raised from default after the MD prices the abandoned-basket risk), staff purchase enabled at the flagship only, wired to the payroll component per the Payroll track's law.</p><p><b>The voucher tab and the approvers (Ch. 7-8).</b> Tab four is left DELIBERATELY conservative for module 7's launch: master switch off until the programme's accounts exist (the accountant's condition, correct), the launch design pre-agreed — Online Only, booking enforcement ON for the multi-till flagship, buy-back OFF permanently. Tab five stays empty (no integrated terminals yet — the card machine keys in manually, recorded as a decision not an omission). The approver table lands per module 1's casting: four approvers, boxes ticked per person (returns NOT bundled with discount; credit-limit override held by two only), and the go-live checklist's override rehearsal catches the module's named failure live: the second Abeokuta shop's profile required return overrides with NO approver row — the disabled-control-and-told state reproduced on the test till, the row added, the check earning its place on the permanent checklist. First sign-ins verify the landing consoles; the sidebar mirrors the constitution; and the MD's note closes the module: <i>we argued for an afternoon about checkboxes and I realised at some point we were writing the shop's laws. Good. They were overdue.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 10 — Common mistakes & the second law", "13", "<p>The constitution's scar tissue. Symptom → disease → fix.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the inherited constitution.</b> Symptom: nobody can say which discount mode runs or why; defaults everywhere; the most permissive accident governing the margin. Disease: the profile configured by momentum, never decided (Ch. 1, 5). Fix: the walk — every consequential field converted from accident to decision, owned, recorded, and re-walked annually.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — groundwork skipped.</b> Symptom: the profile built in ten minutes and the postings unpicked for a week; the group warehouse, the wrong company, the missing tax template. Disease: the ten records assumed instead of verified (Ch. 2). Fix: the checklist in order, the sanity-check sale end to end from the back office, and the foundation priced before the furniture.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the shared template rewrite.</b> Symptom: two counters' tax behaviour flipping mysteriously; each profile save undoing the other's. Disease: opposite tax treatments pointed at one template (Ch. 3). Fix: one template, one behaviour — each treatment its own record, and the blast radius of shared-record settings said out loud before any toggle.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the validity-window leak.</b> Symptom: items ringing up at nil; the till flagging and the floor ignoring; margin sold at zero while everyone assumes a price. Disease: rates with lapsed windows treated as mysteries (Ch. 3). Fix: the zero-rated scan as a standing first-check, the price list's windows reviewed on selling's calendar, and the flag treated as the alarm it is.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the unguarded reverse gear.</b> Symptom: cash refunds on from day one with no override; the drawer open in both directions; the phantom return's mechanism pre-installed. Disease: returns configured by sympathy instead of policy (Ch. 6). Fix: store-credit-first as the launch posture, cash refund as a deliberate later step with its override companion, the policy window enforced with a governed gate.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — no approvers, no override.</b> Symptom: the discount control disabled at 6 p.m. on launch day; the queue, the scene, the corridor toggle offered as first aid. Disease: override paths required but never staffed or rehearsed (Ch. 8). Fix: at least one approver per required permission, and every override path tested with a real approval on the go-live checklist before the first customer.</p><p><b>The second law.</b> The counter met its system; now it has its constitution: <b>every counter power is a profile decision with a named owner — the groundwork verified before the record exists, the tabs walked field by field, the discount mode chosen never inherited, money's reverse gear guarded, the voucher tab conservative by design, the access rows normalised and the approver table staffed and rehearsed — written where the machinery enforces it.</b> Module 3 takes the constitution to the metal: commissioning the terminals and governing the estate.</p>"),
]

